C2 phase 4 — the stateless querier (CL-3)
2026-08-10 · router + ingester×2 + querier×2 + one S3 bucket
             (bench/compose/timelakedb-cluster-s3.yml)
================================================================================

THE CLAIM UNDER TEST is the one the whole role split rests on: separating
reads from the write path costs neither freshness nor exactness. A querier
owns no data. It replays the catalog from the shared bucket, tails the
manifest log, and unions the ingesters' LIVE BUFFERS on top — because
seconds after ingest the rows are still in an ingester's memory, in no
Parquet file and no catalog, and AT-2 demands exact counts there.

This is also the first rig where two writers commit to one manifest log, so
the P0-4 catalog CAS runs under real contention rather than a unit test.

Drilled: 3,200 lines across 8 tables, written through the single router
endpoint and sharded across the ingester pair.

  19/19 checks passed

FRESHNESS — the answer is exact while the rows exist ONLY in memory.
  - Both queriers returned all 3,200 rows with NOTHING flushed
    (timelake_parquet_files contributed nothing for these tables).
  - 38,400 rows crossed the wire as live buffer snapshots
    (timelake_querier_snapshot_rows_total) — proof the answer came from the
    ingesters' memory, not from files.
  - querier-a and querier-b agreed exactly. Stateless nodes are
    interchangeable, or they are not stateless.
  - The router's forwarded query gave the same answer as asking a querier
    directly.
  - Sharding evidence a shared catalog cannot blur: each ingester's OWN
    live buffer (/internal/v1/live) held 4 of the 8 tables — every table on
    exactly one primary, distributed across the pair.

THE FLUSH — rows cross from memory to the bucket with no loss and no
double-count. This is the window that can only fail one way: a querier
whose catalog view is older than a commit, reading a buffer the rows have
already left, would show neither copy. Closed by a watermark — every
internal response carries the serving ingester's catalog head, read AFTER
its buffers, and the querier folds the manifest log forward to that head
before it reads any file list. A batch missing from a snapshot is therefore
guaranteed to be below the watermark, hence visible.
  - The querier followed the ingesters' commits (catalog head advanced).
  - Counts stayed EXACT across the transition, on both queriers.

FAILURE BEHAVIOUR
  - Kill a querier: reads continue on the other, and the ROUTER falls
    through to it rather than failing every other query. (Round-robin with
    no fall-through turns one dead node into a 50% error rate; the router
    health-checks nothing — CL-5 — so "is it up" is answered by asking.)
  - Restart it: exact again.
  - CL-4, a querier rebuilt from an EMPTY disk: `up --force-recreate` on a
    volume-less container reconstructed the entire view from the bucket and
    answered exactly. The queriers carry no volumes on purpose, so this is
    a property of the deployment, not an assertion in a document.
  - Kill an INGESTER and the querier REFUSES the query (HTTP 400, named
    error, timelake_querier_refusals_total +1) rather than answering short.
    This is the deliberate opposite of the write path's PR-7 trade: a write
    that degrades is still honest about what it stored; a query that
    degrades lies. Counts returned, exact, once the ingester was back.
  - A write sent to a querier is refused with 501 — not 400 (nothing is
    wrong with the request) and not 500 (nothing is broken). Accepting it
    would acknowledge a write that is durable nowhere the cluster reads.

WHAT THE DRILL CAUGHT (both fixed before this log was written)
  1. A table written a moment ago was invisible for up to a second. The
     querier listed tables from a view the background tail refreshed on a
     tick, so a BRAND-NEW table — in no catalog, in no local buffer — was
     "table not found" until the tick landed. Write-then-read is exactly
     the case the freshness claim is about, so the live view is now
     refreshed ON THE QUERY PATH, before the table list is taken, with the
     returned head folding the catalog forward in the same step. Steady
     state costs one LAN round trip per ingester and no store calls.
  2. A restarted peer left dead sockets in the querier's connection pool,
     and the first use of one failed instantly — surfacing as "the cluster
     is incomplete" for a blip that heals itself. Snapshot reads are
     idempotent, so they now retry once; a genuinely absent peer still
     refuses the connection immediately and fails fast.

CLUSTER SMOKE — the unmodified harness drives the whole cluster through the
router's single address, and gets the SAME numbers as one node:

    python bench.py run --backend timelakedb --url http://localhost:5970 \
                        --scale smoke
    (bench/results/timelakedb-cl3-cluster-through-router-20260810-203730/)

  - ingest 323,269 lines/s, 0 errors — writes sharded across the pair;
  - Shape A median 58 ms / p95 68 ms, 0 errors;
  - all five Shape B queries completed;
  - context rows_48h = 77,806 EXACT — the same value the single-node run
    produces, now composed by a querier unioning two ingesters' live
    buffers with the shared bucket, reached through a router. host_rows_6h
    = 3,000 and disk_rows_6h = 21,000, both exact.
  - burst 5,000 events, 0 errors, concurrent query 22 ms.
  - (B4 returned 472 hourly buckets against the single node's 473: that
    count follows the wall-clock hour boundaries of a 48 h window at run
    time, not the data. Storage is unmeasurable through the router, which
    has no data directory to size — correctly so.)

FR-8/FR-9 therefore survive the role split: the bench adapter, Telegraf and
Grafana see one address and never learn the cluster exists.

REGRESSION — the earlier phases and the single-node default are unchanged.
  - CL-2 replication drill (phase 2): 12/12 PASS.
  - Router sharding drill (phase 3): 8/8 PASS, including the 501 for
    /api/sql on a router with no queriers configured.
  - role=all bench smoke: 296,885 lines/s, 0 errors, Shape A median 49 ms,
    all five Shape B complete, context rows_48h = 77,806 exact
    (bench/results/timelakedb-cl3-regression-20260810-203229/run.json).

SCOPE / NOT YET
  - Flight SQL is served BY a querier (Grafana points at one) but is not
    forwarded BY the router — the router speaks HTTP only.
  - A query registers providers for every table in the database, so it
    fans out snapshot requests for tables it will not read. Correct but
    wasteful; narrowing it to the tables a query references is a C3
    refinement.
  - Intra-cluster links (replication, live snapshots) are plaintext HTTP at
    C2 and move behind required mTLS at C3. /internal/v1/snapshot returns
    ROWS, so that listener is the same trust boundary as read access to the
    bucket and must never be public.
  - Automatic failover of a dead ingester's shard still needs health
    (phase 5 territory, with the compactor lease).

WORKSPACE: 164 tests (+23: query ipc round-trip incl. dictionary survival,
querier live-report parsing / peer ordering / refusal / outage tolerance,
router querier rotation + fall-through, and 9 two-engine integration tests
that wire an ingester and a querier to one store in a single process),
clippy clean, fmt clean.
